  "id": "Compound::DB01115 - Gene::776",
  "verbalization": "Nifedipine, a calcium channel blocker, is linked to Cacna1d through a series of molecular interactions that are mediated by the broader class of dihydropyridine compounds. The evidence suggests that Nifedipine, as a member of the dihydropyridine family, binds to Cacna1d, which is a key subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels. This relationship is supported by the shared ancestry of Nifedipine and other dihydropyridines, such as Clevidipine and Felodipine, which all converge at the dihydropyridine class. The binding of these compounds to Cacna1d is consistent across multiple pathways, indicating a general mechanism of action involving calcium channel modulation. The presence of multiple pathways converging on Cacna1d underscores the importance of this gene in the pharmacological effects of dihydropyridine drugs, and suggests that Nifedipine's interaction with Cacna1d is a central feature of its therapeutic profile.",
